{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1104748-sell-me-on-this-game","id":1104748,"title":"Sell me on this game","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mage: The Awakening"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-06-17T09:31:40-07:00","id":1104748,"title":"Sell me on this game","content":"Mage has always been the black sheep of Chronicles of darkness to me. I have played Vampire, Werewolf, changelig and Hunter. I haveand read the books for the other games and like them (but haven't had the opportunity to play). But mage is a game I have managed to &quot;get&quot;.<br /> <br /> I think it is a matter of focus. All the other games have themes that I can follow enough to get nice stories and characters out of it. But with mage I find the themes a bit, nebulous. The themes of duty and controlling yourself found in werewolf speak to me very much. But with mage I struggle to find a theme I can identify with. <br /> <br /> I also struggle to think up aims for mage as well. I am not sure what I should be trying to do in mage. Most of the other games have things you need to be doing in order to just carry on with your characters life. But I don't see what stops a mage just carrying on like nothing happened (their lives seem easier than before, e.g. make the barrista think you already paid for your coffee etc). <br /> <br /> Could you guys give me some examples of what sort of problems and stories you have come up with for his game? Perhaps tell me about what part of his game you find easier to in vest in.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12311-freezze12","name":"freezze12","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-17T12:07:36-07:00","id":1104810,"title":"","content":"If it helps, Second Edition's theme is Addicted to Mysteries. Mages are constantly aware of the supernatural around them, always conscious of it, and they're driven to investigate, to figure it out. to develop their Gnosis and so investigate greater mysteries.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/51-marin","name":"marin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2019"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-17T12:11:57-07:00","id":1104813,"title":"","content":"Mages are the 1%, what do you think gets them out of bed every day?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-17T12:39:44-07:00","id":1104824,"title":"","content":"Hmm...ok, first off I'm going to list your requests and statements for my own organizational purposes.<br /> <br /> 1. You have difficulty &quot;getting&quot; mage.<br /> 2. Mage's themes seem nebulous.<br /> 3. You struggle with the idea of purpose for individual characters.<br /> 4. You want to know examples of problems that mages face.<br /> 5. You want to know what is easy to invest, or get excited about the game.<br /> 6. Life seems too easy for mages.<br /> <br /> <br /> Ok, so, first off this question has been asked multiple times before. The reason I say that is that normally I don't tell people that they should go digging on their own for information, but others have answered this question better than I will. Other people have also given more thorough answers before. So the first thing I would say is that you might want to look at previous &quot;sell me on this game&quot; threads. They may have answers to questions that are at the back of your mind but haven't come to the front yet.<br /> <br /> Secondly, I will say that not every game is for everyone. I personally do not enjoy Vampire. It's possible that Mage isn't the game for you.<br /> <br /> Now that that is out of the way, I can move forward with why I enjoy the game. Mage has the most comprehensive free form magic system out there. If you want to come up with a spell, there's some combination that can likely do what you're looking to do, even if your character does not yet possess the capability to cast it. The game is also about figuring out supernatural mysteries. I like that theme (technically the theme is Addicted to Mysteries, but you get what I mean). I can think of what I would do if I were a mage when it comes to my daily life, but every Mystery presents a new adventure that I can't account for until it happens. The onus is really on both the player and the ST to express why the character must pursue any given mystery.<br /> <br /> As to conflict and problems, Mage abounds with them. You have other mages that don't want you to have access to certain mysteries, other mages that hate you just for existing, other mages that see you as a resource (reapers), supernatural phenomena that is dangerous to study, other supernatural creatures that don't like the fact that you can easily discover their existence, obsessions that drive you to do things that you would consider abhorrent just so that you can feel the satisfaction of understanding THAT mystery that's been just beyond your reach for the past six years...etc.<br /> <br /> Magic makes things more complicated, not more simple (typically). Yes, you can make that barrista forget that you haven't paid for your coffee, but if you keep on using magic to do things that go against wisdom you can slide into having very low wisdom (or even becoming one of the mad). Every time you cast a spell for a purpose that goes against another mage, you create potential conflict. Now that you have power, you have to defend yourself against spirits which will gather around such fonts of resources. Now that you're a mage you can see how the goetia in your oneiros affect the way you think, making you question whether or not you've ever had an independent thought.<br /> <br /> I'd also like to point out the fact that a mage's peripheral mage sight makes the existence of the supernatural obvious to mages. That's not a comforting thought. That alone makes a mage's life more complicated. How do you know that what just pinged your senses isn't something heading your way to eat your heart out because of how you treated that one weirdo the other day? How do you know that what just pinged your senses isn't another mage trying to read your memories so he can discover how you hid your grimoire? EVERY supernatural thing that pings your senses could potentially be a threat in some form or fashion. That very easily leads to paranoia, especially when so much of the supernatural world IS dangerous.<br /> <br /> I'll agree that the theme of &quot;The world is a lie&quot; is nebulous, but the thing about that is that you have to bring that to life. You have to incorporate how your mage sight causes your character to look at the world differently, how having the ability to bring down phenomena from another world makes that world more real than this one, which in turn causes the way your character treats other people to change.<br /> <br /> All of these things I find interesting. How does your character deal with paranoia? In what way does your character enjoy spellcasting (presuming the character isn't a banisher). What conflicts have arisen because of actions your character did (or did not) take? How has your character shown hubris? How has your character shown restraint and or creativity? What particular phenomena is your character interested in, and why?<br /> <br /> <br /> Hopefully what I've written helps.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7825-falcon777","name":"Falcon777","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"490"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-17T12:55:20-07:00","id":1104830,"title":"","content":"The core aspect of Mage (going well back to the WoD) is that mages are nearly godlike in their potential power, but they're still humans in terms of their minds. It's all about exploring the hubris of such limited beings having such vast power at their disposal. Part of the dilemma with this as a game, is that it is much more individualistic. &quot;What do you do with cosmic power&quot; doesn't lend itself to the more direct themes of many of the other game-lines.<br /> <br /> As noted above, Mage 2e seeks to address this by focusing on the addictive nature of power. Mages are addicts, they're obsessed, they spend every waking moment (and possibly many others) aware that the world around them is a Lie and they have the power to see through the Lie. And every time they pierce a bit deeper into the truth they're not punished for their transgression, they're rewarded with greater power, and greater understanding... and of course the trap of getting drawn further and further into the Mysteries.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-17T13:32:47-07:00","id":1104845,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I also struggle to think up aims for mage as well. I am not sure what I should be trying to do in mage. </div> </div> </div>At a basic level, that's what Orders are for, they each have an agenda. If you're starting out, a fairly basic plot is to have an Order simply give your players a task.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Most of the other games have things you need to be doing in order to just carry on with your characters life. But I don't see what stops a mage just carrying on like nothing happened (their lives seem easier than before, e.g. make the barrista think you already paid for your coffee etc). </div> </div> </div>It's not that different to Vampire. Hypothetically a Kindred could just keep their head down and live their life, the difficulty is that other Kindred interfere. A covenant is at least partially a way to make other Kindred back off.<br /> .<br /> The same's more or less true for the Awakened. There are factions in the setting that want your character dead. If you aren't planning to join them, then Banishers and Seers are ideologically committed to murdering you. In those circumstances, it's perhaps easier to see why most Awakened in the setting join an Order, from there it's fairly easy to start getting roped into the ideology and agendas of the Orders.<br /> <br /> That's not to say there aren't people in the setting who do that, but it tends to end badly.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-17T15:22:48-07:00","id":1104878,"title":"","content":"More than the other splats Mages are motivated by curiosity. They are DnD style adventurers. Dark castle on the hill, I wonder what they keep in their dungeon? Ooh, shiny, want!<br /> \u200bThe fact that they aren't so preoccupied by mere survival or maintaining the security of their territory leaves them free to chase anything that catches their eye.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6084-2pttakrill","name":"2ptTakrill","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"942"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-19T00:40:22-07:00","id":1105303,"title":"","content":"Well it also depends on what kind of storytelling you find engaging <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12311-freezze12\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"12311\" data-vbnamecard=\"12311\">freezze12</a>. If you don't find a glimmer of interest in Mage now, you might not enjoy it later on when you're attempting to decipher the mechanics - and neither will your players.<br /> <br /> If I may suggest, read the Fallen World Anthology available in DriveThruRPG. You get snippets of Mage stories that could provide the aha moment. Good luck!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4260-terpsichordean","name":"terpsichordean","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4260_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"79"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-19T08:43:03-07:00","id":1105407,"title":"","content":"The mechanics are daunting for most people, but ultimately rewarding as you can build almost any spell you can imagine.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1470-johnny-awesome","name":"Johnny Awesome","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1470_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"447"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}